LIB_LOAD("ms",{"ms1":{"challenge":[{"q":"In which year was Mary Slessor born?","o":["1848","1948","1748","1876"],"why":"In Scotland."},{"q":"Which city did Mary's family move to?","o":["Dundee","Edinburgh","Calabar","London"],"why":"When she was ten."},{"q":"What problem did Mary's father have?","o":["Drinking too much","Gambling on horses","Laziness","Nothing"],"why":"It made home life hard."},{"q":"Who first told Mary about Calabar?","o":["Her mother","Her teacher","A sailor","David Livingstone"],"why":"She loved missionary news."},{"q":"How many hours a day did Mary work at fourteen?","o":["About ten or more","One","Two","Twenty-four"],"why":"Weaving at a loom."},{"q":"What did Mary do after work?","o":["Taught Sunday school and helped a mission","Went dancing","Slept all evening","Nothing"],"why":"She served even when tired."},{"q":"What did the gang leader do?","o":["Swung a lead weight round her head","Stole her book","Pushed her into a river","Called her names only"],"why":"She didn't flinch."},{"q":"When did David Livingstone's death become news in Scotland?","o":["1874","1848","1915","1900"],"why":"It inspired Mary."},{"q":"How old was Mary when she sailed to Africa?","o":["Twenty-seven","Eleven","Fifty","Eighteen"],"why":"In August 1876."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"God hath not given us the spirit of ___.\"","o":["fear","joy","love","power"],"why":"2 Timothy 1:7."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did Mary prop on her loom so she could learn while she worked?","o":["A book","A mirror","A radio","A sandwich"],"why":"One line at a time."},"choices":[{"title":"Hard at home","q":"Things at home are difficult right now, and you feel embarrassed when friends ask about your family.","o":[{"t":"Talk to God and a trusted adult about it, and keep doing your best. Your home doesn't define your future.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary's hard home didn't stop God from using her mightily."},{"t":"Lie and pretend everything is perfect.","f":"You don't have to share everything, but you don't have to carry it alone."},{"t":"Give up on school because 'what's the point'.","f":"Mary kept learning, even on a loom."}]},{"title":"The bully's test","q":"Some older boys block your way and try to scare you into giving up your lunch money.","o":[{"t":"Stay calm, say no clearly, walk away, and report it to a teacher.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Calm courage and getting help are both wise."},{"t":"Start a fight.","f":"Mary didn't fight. Calm courage is stronger."},{"t":"Give them money every day from now on.","f":"That just feeds the bullying."}]},{"title":"Too ordinary?","q":"Your church needs volunteers to help with the children's programme. You think: \"Me? I'm nobody special.\"","o":[{"t":"Say, \"Here am I,\" and sign up.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! God used a poor mill girl to change Calabar."},{"t":"Wait for someone more talented.","f":"God looks for willing hearts, not perfect ones."},{"t":"Say you're too busy, even though you're not.","f":"Mary worked ten-hour days and still served."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"dundee","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 1 of Mary Slessor's story in real life."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"🏚️ Your home doesn't decide your future. God sees you."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"🧵 Keep learning in creative ways, even when life is busy and hard."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"💪 Face fear with calm courage and love."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"🚢 God wants willing hearts. Say, 'Here am I; send me.'"}],"stops":{"ms1s1":{"story":[{"sc":"dundee","n":"Mary Slessor was born in Scotland in 1848. When she was ten, her family moved to {Dundee}, a grey, smoky city full of mills."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Her family was very poor. They lived in a tiny, crowded flat. And life at home was hard, because Mary's father had a drinking problem."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Often he spent the family's money on alcohol, and came home late and angry. Mary and her brothers and sisters learned what it was to be hungry and afraid."},{"sc":"dundee","w":"marymum","e":"worried","t":"Come, children. Whatever happens, we will pray. God sees us."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Mary's mother was a quiet, praying woman. She loved Jesus, and she loved to hear news about missionaries in a far-off place called {Calabar}, in Africa."},{"sc":"dundee","w":"marygirl","e":"big","t":"Mother, tell me about Calabar again! Are there really rivers there with crocodiles?"},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Little Mary would play at being a missionary, teaching imaginary children in the corner of the room. Nobody knew that one day the game would come true."}],"quote":{"text":"A father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows, is God in his holy habitation.","who":"Psalm 68:5","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"God works in hard homes","text":"Mary grew up poor, in a home hurt by her father's drinking. But a praying mother planted seeds of faith and a love for faraway people. If your home is hard, God sees you, and he can grow something beautiful there."},"think":"Mary's mother prayed with her children even when life at home was hard. Who has planted seeds of faith in your life?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did Mary Slessor grow up?","o":["Dundee, in Scotland","Calabar","London","Lagos"],"why":"A grey, smoky mill city."},{"q":"Why was life at home hard?","o":["The family was poor and her father had a drinking problem","They were rich but bored","They lived on a farm","They travelled a lot"],"why":"Hunger and fear."},{"q":"What did Mary's mother love to hear about?","o":["Missionaries in Calabar","Football","Kings and queens","Fashion"],"why":"A far-off place in Africa."},{"q":"What game did little Mary play?","o":["Being a missionary teaching children","Being a pirate","Being a queen","Being a soldier"],"why":"One day it would come true."},{"q":"What does Psalm 68:5 call God?","o":["A father of the fatherless","A distant king","An angry judge","A stranger"],"why":"God sees children in hard homes."}]},"ms1s2":{"story":[{"sc":"dundee","n":"When Mary was eleven, she started work in a huge {jute} mill. She was a {half-timer}: half the day in the mill, half the day at school."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"The mill was hot, dusty and deafeningly loud. Rows and rows of machines clattered all day long."},{"sc":"jutemill","w":"millboss","e":"stern","t":"Faster, girl! Keep that loom running!"},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"By the time she was fourteen, Mary was working full-time at a {loom}, weaving cloth for ten hours a day or more. Her wages helped feed the whole family."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"But Mary was hungry to learn. She propped a book up on her loom and read a few lines whenever she could, while the machine worked."},{"sc":"jutemill","w":"marygirl","e":"smile","t":"One line at a time. One page at a time. I'll learn, whatever it takes."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"After long days at the mill, she taught Sunday school and helped with a mission in the poorest streets of Dundee."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"Those hard years made Mary tough, patient and hard-working. Without knowing it, she was being trained for Africa."}],"quote":{"text":"Whatsoever thy hand findeth to do, do it with thy might.","who":"Ecclesiastes 9:10","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Faithful in hard work","text":"Mary worked ten-hour days at eleven to fourteen years old, and still found ways to learn and serve. Hard, ordinary work can build strength and character for whatever God calls you to next."},"think":"Mary read books propped on her loom. What creative ways could you find to keep learning, even when you're busy?","proverb":{"text":"Hannu da yawa maganin aiki.","lang":"Hausa","mean":"Many hands are the cure for work. Helping one another makes hard work lighter."},"quiz":[{"q":"How old was Mary when she started work in the mill?","o":["Eleven","Five","Twenty","Sixteen"],"why":"As a half-timer."},{"q":"What is a half-timer?","o":["A child who worked half the day and went to school half the day","A part-time teacher","A clock maker","A football player"],"why":"Common in Scotland then."},{"q":"What did the mill make?","o":["Jute cloth for sacks and rope","Cars","Chocolate","Shoes"],"why":"Jute is a rough plant fibre."},{"q":"How did Mary keep learning at work?","o":["She propped a book on her loom","She skipped work","She went to university","She didn't"],"why":"One line at a time."},{"q":"What does Ecclesiastes 9:10 tell us?","o":["Whatever you do, do it with your might","Rest all day","Avoid hard work","Only work for money"],"why":"Do it with all your strength."}]},"ms1s3":{"story":[{"sc":"dundee","n":"Mary helped at a mission for the roughest children in Dundee. But a gang of teenage boys hated it, and tried to frighten the helpers away."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"One evening, the gang surrounded Mary in the street. Their leader had a heavy lead weight tied to a string."},{"sc":"dundee","w":"ganglead","e":"smirk","t":"Let's see how brave the mission lady is."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"He began to swing the weight round and round her head, closer and closer. If it hit her, it could badly hurt her."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Mary stood completely still. She didn't run. She didn't scream. She looked him straight in the eye."},{"sc":"dundee","w":"mary","e":"smile","t":"Go on, then. But when you've finished, you're all coming to the meeting."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"The weight swung so close it nearly touched her face. Then the boy stopped. He let it drop."},{"sc":"dundee","w":"ganglead","e":"shock","t":"She's game, lads. She didn't even flinch. Fine. We'll come."},{"sc":"church","n":"And they did. The gang came to the meeting. Some of them became Mary's loyal friends and helpers. Courage had won them over."}],"quote":{"text":"For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind.","who":"2 Timothy 1:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Calm courage","text":"Mary didn't fight the gang or run away. She stood calmly, and her courage won their respect. Bullies often expect fear. Calm courage, with love, can surprise them, and even change them."},"think":"Why do you think Mary's calm courage changed the gang more than shouting or running would have?","quiz":[{"q":"Who tried to frighten Mary?","o":["A gang of teenage boys","Her father","A dog","The mill overseer"],"why":"They hated the mission."},{"q":"What did the gang leader swing round her head?","o":["A lead weight on a string","A stick","A rope","A bag"],"why":"Closer and closer."},{"q":"What did Mary do?","o":["Stood completely still and looked him in the eye","Ran away","Screamed","Fought back"],"why":"Calm courage."},{"q":"What happened afterwards?","o":["The gang came to the meeting, and some became her helpers","They hurt her","They called the police","She quit"],"why":"Courage won them over."},{"q":"What has God given us instead of fear, according to 2 Timothy 1:7?","o":["Power, love and a sound mind","Money","Muscles","Luck"],"why":"Not the spirit of fear."}]},"ms1s4":{"story":[{"sc":"dundee","n":"In 1874, news reached Scotland that the great explorer and missionary {David Livingstone} had died in Africa."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"All over Scotland, people asked: who will take his place? Who will go to Africa now?"},{"sc":"dundee","w":"mary","e":"big","t":"I will. I'll go. I want to go to Calabar."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"It seemed impossible. Mary was a poor mill girl with little schooling. She was a woman, at a time when few women went. And her family needed her wages."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"But she applied to her church's mission. They accepted her! She studied for a while in Edinburgh, and arranged for part of her wages to be sent home to her mother."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"In August 1876, aged twenty-seven, Mary sailed from Liverpool on a ship heading for West Africa."},{"sc":"seashore","w":"mary","e":"smile","t":"Lord, I'm only a mill girl. But here am I. Send me."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"As Scotland disappeared behind her, Mary had no idea of the adventures ahead: rivers, forests, chiefs, warriors, and many, many children who would call her 'Ma'."}],"quote":{"text":"Also I heard the voice of the Lord, saying, Whom shall I send, and who will go for us? Then said I, Here am I; send me.","who":"Isaiah 6:8","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Ordinary, available","text":"Mary was not rich, famous or highly educated. But she was available. When God asked 'Who will go?', she said, 'Send me.' God isn't looking for perfect people. He's looking for willing ones."},"think":"What are some reasons people give for why they can't serve God? What reasons might Mary have had?","quiz":[{"q":"Whose death inspired Mary to go to Africa?","o":["David Livingstone's","Queen Victoria's","Her father's","A king's"],"why":"News reached Scotland in 1874."},{"q":"Why did it seem impossible for Mary to go?","o":["She was poor, had little schooling, and her family needed her wages","She was too old","She couldn't swim","She didn't like travelling"],"why":"But she applied anyway."},{"q":"How did Mary keep helping her family?","o":["Part of her wages was sent home to her mother","She took them with her","She didn't","She sold her house"],"why":"She still cared for them."},{"q":"When did Mary sail for Africa?","o":["August 1876","January 1900","June 1822","May 1950"],"why":"Aged twenty-seven."},{"q":"What did Isaiah say when God asked who would go?","o":["Here am I; send me","Send someone else","Not me","Later"],"why":"Mary said the same."}]}}},"ms2":{"challenge":[{"q":"Which river did Mary sail up to reach Calabar?","o":["The Calabar River","The Niger","The Nile","The Thames"],"why":"Lined with mangroves."},{"q":"What colour was Mary's hair?","o":["Red","Black","Blonde","Grey"],"why":"The children noticed!"},{"q":"What is Efik?","o":["The people and language of Calabar","A kind of fish","A city in Scotland","A disease"],"why":"Mary learned it well."},{"q":"Why did Mary live in a mud house?","o":["To live among the people and save money for her mother","She had no choice","Mud houses are cold","She was hiding"],"why":"Love comes close."},{"q":"What does 'Ma' mean in Calabar?","o":["Mother","Teacher","Stranger","Queen"],"why":"A word of love and respect."},{"q":"What is a superstition?","o":["A belief based on fear rather than truth","A kind of food","A Bible story","A song"],"why":"Like the fear of twins."},{"q":"Who did Mary take in, along with the twin babies?","o":["Their mothers","Their chiefs","Nobody","Soldiers"],"why":"They had nowhere else to go."},{"q":"What did Mary name her adopted daughter?","o":["Janie","Afala","Lygia","Christiana"],"why":"A rescued twin girl."},{"q":"How did people's minds about twins change?","o":["Slowly, as they saw rescued twins grow up healthy","Overnight","Never","By a law from the King"],"why":"Living proof."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Defend the poor and ___.\"","o":["fatherless","rich","strong","famous"],"why":"Psalm 82:3."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What names do Yoruba people often give twins?","o":["Taiwo and Kehinde","Tom and Jerry","Ade and Ola","John and James"],"why":"Twins are a blessing!"},"choices":[{"title":"The one everyone avoids","q":"There's a pupil in your school whom everyone avoids because of a rumour that she's 'bad luck'.","o":[{"t":"Refuse to believe the rumour. Be her friend and include her.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Like Mary, challenge the fear and love the person."},{"t":"Avoid her too, just in case.","f":"That's letting fear win over love."},{"t":"Spread the rumour further.","f":"Rumours like that hurt real people."}]},{"title":"A new language","q":"A family from another part of Nigeria moves next door. Their children are still learning your language.","o":[{"t":"Learn a few words of their language and help them learn yours.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary learned Efik to love people well."},{"t":"Laugh at their accent.","f":"Mary made mistakes learning Efik too."},{"t":"Ignore them until they speak properly.","f":"Love comes close first."}]},{"title":"Simple living","q":"Your friends all want expensive trainers. You have enough, but you feel pressure to ask your parents for the latest ones.","o":[{"t":"Be content with what you have, and maybe use some savings to help someone in need.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary lived simply so she could give more."},{"t":"Beg your parents until they give in.","f":"Things don't make you worth more."},{"t":"Mock friends who have older shoes.","f":"That's the opposite of Mary's way."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 2 of Mary Slessor's story in real life."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"🗣️ Learn other people's languages and ways. It's a powerful way to love them."},{"sc":"hut","n":"🏠 Love comes close. Live simply and share generously."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"👶 Defend the smallest and weakest. Every child is precious to God."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"💗 Be living proof of the truth: patient love changes minds over time."}],"stops":{"ms2s1":{"story":[{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"In September 1876, Mary's ship sailed up the Calabar River. Mangrove trees lined the banks. The air was hot and heavy."},{"sc":"calabarriver","w":"mary","e":"big","t":"Calabar! At last! It's even more beautiful than Mother described."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"She lived at first at the mission in {Duke Town}, part of old Calabar. There were schools, a church and other missionaries."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"The heat was overwhelming. Fevers came often. Mosquitoes buzzed all night. Many missionaries got sick, and some died."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"But Mary loved the people from the start: the busy markets, the singing, the children who followed her everywhere."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"efikchild","e":"big","t":"The new white woman has red hair! Come and see!"},{"sc":"palaver","n":"She got to work learning {Efik}, the language of Calabar. She listened, practised, made mistakes, laughed, and practised again."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Within a short time she could speak it so well that people were amazed. Some said she spoke Efik as well as they did."}],"quote":{"text":"I am made all things to all men, that I might by all means save some.","who":"1 Corinthians 9:22","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Learn their language","text":"Mary didn't expect the people of Calabar to learn English for her. She learned Efik for them. When you want to love people well, you meet them where they are: their language, their food, their way of life."},"think":"Why do you think learning someone's language shows them love and respect?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Mary arrive in Calabar?","o":["September 1876","August 1848","June 1822","January 1915"],"why":"Up the Calabar River."},{"q":"Where did she live at first?","o":["The mission in Duke Town","A palace","A ship","The forest"],"why":"With other missionaries."},{"q":"What dangers did missionaries face?","o":["Heat and fevers","Snow","Earthquakes","Volcanoes"],"why":"Many got sick."},{"q":"What language did Mary learn?","o":["Efik","Hausa","French","Latin"],"why":"The language of Calabar."},{"q":"How well did she learn it?","o":["So well people were amazed","Not at all","Only a few words","She needed an interpreter always"],"why":"She practised and practised."}]},"ms2s2":{"story":[{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"After a few years, Mary asked to move out of the main mission station to live in a village further along the river."},{"sc":"hut","n":"She didn't live like a rich foreigner. She moved into a simple mud house. She ate the same food as her neighbours: yams, plantains, fish and pepper soup."},{"sc":"hut","w":"mary","e":"smile","t":"If I eat what they eat and live as they live, I can save more of my pay to send home to Mother, and I'll understand the people better too."},{"sc":"hut","n":"She often walked barefoot, didn't fuss about fancy clothes, and went without the comforts other missionaries thought they needed."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"People began calling her 'Ma'. In Calabar, that's a word of deep love and respect: mother."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"villageelder","e":"smile","t":"This white woman is not like the others. She sits with us. She eats our food. She is our Ma."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Mary didn't try to make the people of Calabar into Scots. She loved them as they were, and showed them Jesus in their own way of life."}],"quote":{"text":"And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us.","who":"John 1:14","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Love comes close","text":"Jesus didn't love us from far away. He came and lived among us. Mary followed his example: she lived among the people, ate their food and shared their lives. Real love comes close."},"think":"Why do you think people trusted Mary more because she lived like them?","quiz":[{"q":"Where did Mary choose to live?","o":["In a simple mud house in a village","In a big mansion","On a ship","Back in Scotland"],"why":"Among the people."},{"q":"What food did she eat?","o":["The same local food as her neighbours","Only Scottish food","Only tinned food","Nothing"],"why":"Yams, plantains, fish and pepper soup."},{"q":"Why did she live so simply?","o":["To save money for her mother and understand the people better","She was lazy","She was told to","She was lost"],"why":"Love comes close."},{"q":"What did people start calling her?","o":["Ma","Madam","Teacher","Stranger"],"why":"Mother: a word of love and respect."},{"q":"What does John 1:14 say Jesus did?","o":["Dwelt among us","Stayed far away","Visited once","Sent a letter"],"why":"Love comes close."}]},"ms2s3":{"story":[{"sc":"palaver","n":"Mary soon discovered something that broke her heart."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"In those days, many people in the area believed a terrible {superstition}: that {twins} were cursed. They feared that one of the twins was the child of an evil spirit."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"So when twins were born, people were terrified. The babies were often left to die in the forest. And the mother was often driven out of her village."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"It's important to understand that most people weren't cruel by nature. They were afraid. They had been taught to fear twins all their lives."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"Mary refused to accept it. Whenever she heard that twins had been born, she ran to rescue them, sometimes through the forest at night."},{"sc":"rainforest","w":"mary","e":"stern","t":"These babies are not cursed. They are precious children made by God. Give them to me."},{"sc":"hut","n":"She took the babies home, fed them, nursed them and loved them. She also took in their mothers, who had nowhere else to go."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Today, in many parts of Nigeria, twins are celebrated. Yoruba people call them {Ibeji} and name them Taiwo and Kehinde with joy. Mary would have loved that."}],"quote":{"text":"Defend the poor and fatherless: do justice to the afflicted and needy.","who":"Psalm 82:3","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Fear is not truth","text":"Many people feared twins because of what they had been taught. Mary showed that every child is precious to God. Some beliefs come from fear, not from God's truth. Love the people, but challenge the fear."},"think":"Are there any fears or superstitions in our communities today that hurt children or vulnerable people?","proverb":{"text":"Ọmọ lẹ̀ṣọ́ ayé.","lang":"Yoruba","mean":"Children are the beauty of the world. Every child is a treasure."},"quiz":[{"q":"What did many people fear about twins in those days?","o":["That they were cursed","That they would be rich","That they would be kings","Nothing"],"why":"A superstition based on fear."},{"q":"What often happened to twin babies?","o":["They were left to die in the forest","They were celebrated","They went to school","They became chiefs"],"why":"People were terrified."},{"q":"What did Mary do?","o":["Rescued the babies and took in their mothers","Ignored it","Agreed with the fear","Left Calabar"],"why":"Every child is precious."},{"q":"Why does the story say most people weren't cruel by nature?","o":["They had been taught to fear twins","They didn't know","They were forced","They were asleep"],"why":"Fear, not hatred."},{"q":"What do Yoruba people call twins today?","o":["Ibeji","Ofe","Oyinbo","Agbada"],"why":"Celebrated as a blessing."}]},"ms2s4":{"story":[{"sc":"rainforest","n":"One day, Mary heard that twins had been born to a woman nearby: a boy and a girl."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"She hurried to find them. Sadly, the baby boy had already died. But the little girl was still alive."},{"sc":"hut","w":"mary","e":"smile","t":"Hello, little one. You're safe now. You'll be my own daughter. I'll call you Janie."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Mary adopted Janie as her own child. Janie grew up healthy, clever and loved, the first of many children Mary adopted."},{"sc":"seashore","n":"When Mary went home to Scotland to recover from fever, she took Janie with her. People in Scotland were amazed to meet a little girl who had been saved from the forest."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"And back in Calabar, something began to change. People saw Janie growing up happy and strong. Nothing terrible happened to her, or to Mary."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"twinmother","e":"worried","t":"Ma, you kept my twins, and no curse came. Maybe the old fear was wrong."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Slowly, very slowly, minds began to change. It took many years. But every rescued twin was a living proof that the fear was false."}],"quote":{"text":"Suffer little children, and forbid them not, to come unto me: for of such is the kingdom of heaven.","who":"Matthew 19:14","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Living proof","text":"Mary didn't only argue that twins weren't cursed. She showed it, by raising Janie happy and healthy. Sometimes the best way to change minds is to live out the truth in front of people, patiently, for years."},"think":"How did Janie's life help change people's minds more than words alone could have?","quiz":[{"q":"What was the name of the twin girl Mary adopted?","o":["Janie","Hannah","Susan","Mary"],"why":"The first of many adopted children."},{"q":"What had happened to her twin brother?","o":["He had already died","He became a chief","He went to Scotland","He ran away"],"why":"Mary arrived too late for him."},{"q":"Where did Mary take Janie when she was sick?","o":["To Scotland","To Lagos","To Egypt","To America"],"why":"To recover from fever."},{"q":"How did Janie help change minds?","o":["She grew up happy and healthy, proving the fear was false","She gave speeches","She became a queen","She didn't"],"why":"Living proof."},{"q":"What did Jesus say about little children?","o":["Let them come to me","Send them away","They don't matter","They are too noisy"],"why":"Matthew 19:14."}]}}},"ms3":{"challenge":[{"q":"What was Okoyong?","o":["A forest district north of Calabar","A city in Scotland","A river","A ship"],"why":"Outsiders feared it."},{"q":"Why did some people say only a man should go?","o":["They thought it was too dangerous","Women can't paddle","Men speak Efik better","It was a rule"],"why":"Mary went anyway."},{"q":"What was the name of the village where Mary first stayed?","o":["Ekenge","Osogun","Duke Town","Abeokuta"],"why":"Chief Edem's village."},{"q":"Who was Chief Edem's sister?","o":["Ma Eme","Janie","Afala","Acte"],"why":"Mary's close friend."},{"q":"How long did Mary stay in Okoyong?","o":["About fifteen years","One week","Fifty years","One year"],"why":"Patience and love."},{"q":"What did Mary do when two villages were about to fight?","o":["Stood between them and got them to talk","Ran away","Joined one side","Watched"],"why":"Brave peacemaking."},{"q":"What did Chief Edem say changed after Ma came?","o":["Now we talk first","Now we fight more","Nothing changed","Now we have guns"],"why":"Before, they fought."},{"q":"What happened when an important person died?","o":["People were accused of witchcraft and tested with poison","There was a party","Nothing","They planted a tree"],"why":"A harsh custom."},{"q":"Who helped Mary while she stood guard?","o":["Ma Eme","Chief Edem's warriors","Queen Victoria","Nobody"],"why":"Friends make courage stronger."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Let us not be weary in well ___.\"","o":["doing","eating","sleeping","talking"],"why":"Galatians 6:9."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What is the poisonous esere bean also called?","o":["The Calabar bean","The jelly bean","The cocoa bean","The coffee bean"],"why":"It grows in the area around Calabar."},"choices":[{"title":"About to fight","q":"Two of your friends are shouting at each other after a football match. Fists are clenched and a crowd is gathering to watch.","o":[{"t":"Step in calmly, separate them, and help them talk it out (or get an adult if it's dangerous).","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Blessed are the peacemakers."},{"t":"Film it for social media.","f":"That makes it worse, not better."},{"t":"Pick a side and join in.","f":"Mary stood between the sides, not with one."}]},{"title":"Someone wrongly blamed","q":"A classmate is being blamed for stealing a phone. You know she was with you the whole time.","o":[{"t":"Speak up clearly and tell the teacher she couldn't have done it.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Like Mary, be a voice for the person being wrongly accused."},{"t":"Stay quiet. It's not your problem.","f":"Your silence could leave an innocent person punished."},{"t":"Tell her privately that you believe her, but say nothing publicly.","f":"Kind, but she needs someone to speak up."}]},{"title":"It's taking too long","q":"You've been trying to get your class to stop littering, and after a month, they still drop rubbish everywhere.","o":[{"t":"Keep going: set an example, find a friend to help, and try a new idea.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary worked fifteen years in Okoyong. Don't grow weary in doing good."},{"t":"Give up. People never change.","f":"People do change, slowly."},{"t":"Shout at everyone.","f":"Patience and example work better."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"rainforest","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 3 of Mary Slessor's story in real life."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"🛶 Go with love to the people others avoid."},{"sc":"hut","n":"⏳ Don't grow weary. Real change grows slowly, like a forest."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"🕊️ Be a brave peacemaker. Help people talk instead of fight."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"⚖️ Speak up for people who have no voice, and keep speaking."}],"stops":{"ms3s1":{"story":[{"sc":"rainforest","n":"North of Calabar lay a forest district called {Okoyong}. Outsiders were afraid of it. Its villages were known for fighting, heavy drinking and harsh customs."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"Missionaries had tried to reach Okoyong before, but it was thought too dangerous. Many said only a man should even try."},{"sc":"calabarriver","w":"mary","e":"stern","t":"Then I'll go. A woman may be safer than a man there. They won't see me as a threat."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"In 1888, Mary set off by canoe, with her adopted children, through rain and darkness, to a village called Ekenge in Okoyong."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"The chief of Ekenge was Chief Edem. He was surprised, but he let her stay. And his sister, Ma Eme, became one of Mary's closest friends."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"maeme","e":"smile","t":"You are brave, Ma, or perhaps a little mad! Come, you will stay with us."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"chiefedem","e":"smile","t":"A white woman, alone, with children? Very well. Let us see what she does."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Mary was the only European for miles. She had no soldiers, no weapons, no money. Just her faith, her love, and her quick tongue in Efik."}],"quote":{"text":"Be strong and of a good courage, fear not, nor be afraid of them: for the LORD thy God, he it is that doth go with thee; he will not fail thee, nor forsake thee.","who":"Deuteronomy 31:6","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Go where others won't","text":"Everyone said Okoyong was too dangerous. Mary went anyway, trusting God. Sometimes the people who most need love are the ones everyone else is afraid of."},"think":"Who are the 'Okoyong' people in your world: the ones others are afraid of or avoid? How could you show them God's love?","quiz":[{"q":"Why were outsiders afraid of Okoyong?","o":["It was known for fighting and harsh customs","It was too cold","It had no water","It was too far north"],"why":"Many thought it too dangerous."},{"q":"Why did Mary think a woman might be safer there?","o":["They wouldn't see her as a threat","Women are stronger","She had soldiers","She was invisible"],"why":"A clever thought."},{"q":"When did Mary go to Okoyong?","o":["1888","1848","1915","1876"],"why":"By canoe, through rain and darkness."},{"q":"Who became one of Mary's closest friends there?","o":["Ma Eme, the chief's sister","Queen Victoria","Janie's mother","A missionary"],"why":"Chief Edem's sister."},{"q":"What did Mary bring with her?","o":["Faith, love and her Efik language, but no soldiers or weapons","An army","Lots of money","Guns"],"why":"Just faith and love."}]},"ms3s2":{"story":[{"sc":"hut","n":"Life in Okoyong was hard. Mary lived in a little hut at first, then helped build her own house with mud and wood, working alongside the villagers."},{"sc":"hut","n":"She treated the sick with simple medicines. She taught children to read. She sat for hours at long village meetings, called a {palaver}, listening and speaking."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"maeme","e":"worried","t":"Ma, be careful tonight. The men have been drinking. There may be trouble."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Ma Eme quietly warned Mary about dangers, and helped her understand the customs. Without her friendship, Mary's work would have been much harder."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Changes came very slowly. Some days nothing seemed to change at all. People listened politely, then did the same old things."},{"sc":"hut","w":"mary","e":"smile","t":"Lord, it's slow. But I'm not giving up. These are your people, and they're my people now too."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Mary stayed in Okoyong for about fifteen years. Bit by bit, through patience and love, trust grew."}],"quote":{"text":"And let us not be weary in well doing: for in due season we shall reap, if we faint not.","who":"Galatians 6:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Don't grow weary","text":"Changing a community takes years, not days. Mary built her house with her own hands, sat through long meetings, and kept loving when nothing seemed to change. Real change grows slowly, like a tree."},"think":"Is there something you've been trying to change (in yourself, your family or your school) that's taking a long time? How does Mary's patience encourage you?","proverb":{"text":"Ofu osisi anaghị eme ọhịa.","lang":"Igbo","mean":"One tree does not make a forest. We need each other, like Mary needed Ma Eme."},"quiz":[{"q":"How did Mary get her house in Okoyong?","o":["She helped build it with mud and wood","She bought a palace","The chief gave her his","She lived on a boat"],"why":"Working alongside the villagers."},{"q":"What is a palaver?","o":["A long meeting to settle a problem","A kind of soup","A canoe","A song"],"why":"Often under a big tree."},{"q":"How did Ma Eme help Mary?","o":["She warned her about dangers and explained customs","She sent her away","She gave her money","She didn't"],"why":"A true friend."},{"q":"How fast did things change in Okoyong?","o":["Very slowly","Overnight","Never","In one week"],"why":"But trust grew."},{"q":"What does the Igbo proverb say?","o":["One tree does not make a forest","Trees are tall","Forests are dangerous","Plant more trees"],"why":"We need each other."}]},"ms3s3":{"story":[{"sc":"palaver","n":"In those days, quarrels between villages in Okoyong often turned into fights, and fights could turn into wars."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"One day, the warriors of one village grabbed their weapons and set off to attack another village. Mary heard and ran after them."},{"sc":"rainforest","w":"okwarrior","e":"frown","t":"Go home, Ma! This is men's business!"},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"Mary didn't go home. She marched straight to the front, between the two armed groups, a small woman standing between angry warriors."},{"sc":"rainforest","w":"mary","e":"stern","t":"Stop! Put down your guns. Let's sit and talk. Nobody needs to die today."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Amazingly, they listened. Because they respected her, both sides agreed to hold a palaver. Sometimes she even had the men hand their guns to her until the talking was done."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Again and again, Mary stopped fights before they started. She became known as a peacemaker that both sides could trust."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"chiefedem","e":"smile","t":"Before Ma came, we fought. Now we talk first."}],"quote":{"text":"Blessed are the peacemakers: for they shall be called the children of God.","who":"Matthew 5:9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Brave peacemaking","text":"Making peace isn't weak or soft. Mary stood between armed warriors and persuaded them to talk. Peacemakers need great courage. Jesus calls them children of God."},"think":"When friends or classmates are about to fight, what could a peacemaker say or do?","quiz":[{"q":"What often happened to quarrels between villages?","o":["They turned into fights and wars","They were forgotten","They became parties","Nothing"],"why":"Until Mary came."},{"q":"Where did Mary stand?","o":["Between the two armed groups","Far behind","In her house","On a tree"],"why":"A small woman between warriors."},{"q":"What did she ask them to do?","o":["Put down their guns and talk","Fight harder","Go home and sleep","Pay her"],"why":"Nobody needs to die."},{"q":"What did she sometimes hold until the talking was done?","o":["The men's guns","Their food","Their children","Their drums"],"why":"They trusted her."},{"q":"What does Jesus call peacemakers?","o":["Children of God","Cowards","Weak","Losers"],"why":"Matthew 5:9."}]},"ms3s4":{"story":[{"sc":"hut","n":"One of the hardest days came when Etim, Chief Edem's son, was badly hurt in an accident. Mary nursed him for days, but he died."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"In Okoyong at that time, when an important person died, people often believed someone had caused the death through {witchcraft}."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Those accused were forced to swallow the {esere bean}, a deadly poison. If they lived, they were called innocent. Most died. And it was the custom to kill people to be buried with an important man."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Soon many people were tied up and waiting to be tested or killed. Mary was horrified."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"mary","e":"stern","t":"These people are innocent! Etim died because of his injury, not because of them. I won't let you kill them!"},{"sc":"palaver","n":"For days and nights, Mary stood guard. She argued. She pleaded. She refused to leave. She was exhausted, but she would not move."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"maeme","e":"worried","t":"Ma, you haven't slept. But you're right. Keep standing. I'll help you."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"In the end, she saved almost all of the prisoners. Over the following years, the poison-bean test and the funeral killings slowly came to an end in Okoyong."}],"quote":{"text":"Open thy mouth for the dumb in the cause of all such as are appointed to destruction. Open thy mouth, judge righteously.","who":"Proverbs 31:8-9","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Voice for the voiceless","text":"The prisoners had no voice and no power. Mary used hers for them, for days, without giving up. When people are being treated unjustly, God calls us to speak up for them, and keep speaking."},"think":"Who doesn't have a voice in our society today? How could you speak up for them?","quiz":[{"q":"Who was Etim?","o":["Chief Edem's son","A missionary","A twin","Mary's brother"],"why":"Mary nursed him, but he died."},{"q":"What was the esere bean?","o":["A deadly poison used to 'test' accused people","A sweet snack","A medicine","A seed for planting"],"why":"Most who swallowed it died."},{"q":"What did Mary say about Etim's death?","o":["He died from his injury, not witchcraft","It was witchcraft","Nobody knows","Someone poisoned him"],"why":"The prisoners were innocent."},{"q":"How long did Mary stand guard?","o":["Days and nights","Five minutes","One hour","She didn't"],"why":"She refused to leave."},{"q":"What happened in the end?","o":["She saved almost all the prisoners, and the customs slowly ended","Everyone died","She was sent away","Nothing changed"],"why":"A voice for the voiceless."}]}}},"ms4":{"challenge":[{"q":"In what year did Mary become vice-consul?","o":["1892","1848","1915","1876"],"why":"The first woman magistrate in the Empire."},{"q":"What kinds of cases did Mary judge?","o":["Land, debts, marriages and stolen goats","Murder in London","Football matches","Nothing"],"why":"Everyday quarrels."},{"q":"How did Mary judge?","o":["With fairness, common sense and humour","Harshly","Randomly","By flipping a coin"],"why":"Justice with mercy."},{"q":"Did Mary ever marry?","o":["No, but she adopted many children","Yes, a chief","Yes, a Scotsman","Yes, a sailor"],"why":"A very big family."},{"q":"Which of these was NOT one of Mary's adopted children?","o":["Ajayi","Janie","Dan","Maggie"],"why":"Ajayi Crowther is from another book!"},{"q":"What did Mary do each time she recovered from fever in Scotland?","o":["Came back to Calabar","Stayed home","Moved to Canada","Wrote a novel"],"why":"She always returned."},{"q":"What did Mary say was nearer than Scotland?","o":["Heaven","The moon","Lagos","Ireland"],"why":"After her family died."},{"q":"Where did Mary move to in her fifties?","o":["Itu and beyond","Dundee","Okoyong only","Abeokuta"],"why":"Further inland."},{"q":"Who did Mary's new settlement help?","o":["Women and girls with nowhere to go","Soldiers","Traders","Nobody"],"why":"To learn skills and earn a living."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"Do justly, and to love ___, and to walk humbly with thy God.\"","o":["mercy","money","power","fame"],"why":"Micah 6:8."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! What did the goat eat in Mary's busy house?","o":["The soap","The Bible","The roof","Dan's homework"],"why":"Never a dull moment!"},"choices":[{"title":"Settling a quarrel","q":"Your two younger siblings are fighting over the TV remote, and your parents ask you to sort it out.","o":[{"t":"Listen to both, make a fair plan (taking turns), and help them make up.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Like Judge Ma: fair, firm and kind."},{"t":"Take the remote for yourself.","f":"That's using power for yourself, not for justice."},{"t":"Side with the one you like more.","f":"A good judge is fair to everyone."}]},{"title":"When you're weak","q":"You've been sick for a week and you're behind on schoolwork. You feel like you'll never catch up.","o":[{"t":"Ask God for strength for today, and do one small piece of work at a time.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary leaned on God for strength one day at a time."},{"t":"Give up on the whole term.","f":"Day by day, you'll catch up."},{"t":"Copy someone else's work.","f":"That doesn't help you learn."}]},{"title":"One more","q":"Your youth group has finished a charity project. Everyone wants to stop, but you know of another family who still needs help.","o":[{"t":"Suggest helping that family too, and offer to lead it.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary always wanted to reach one more village."},{"t":"Say nothing. We've done enough.","f":"Rest is good, but don't forget those still waiting."},{"t":"Complain that nobody else ever helps.","f":"Leaders keep pressing on."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"palaver","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's where you'll use Chapter 4 of Mary Slessor's story in real life."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"⚖️ Do justly, love mercy, and walk humbly with God."},{"sc":"hut","n":"🏠 Make room in your life for people who need a family."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"🌡️ When you're weak, ask God for strength for today."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"🧭 Keep pressing on. There's always one more person who needs help."}],"stops":{"ms4s1":{"story":[{"sc":"palaver","n":"By 1892, the British government had taken control of the area. Its chief officer, Sir Claude MacDonald, visited Okoyong."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"macdonald","e":"smile","t":"Miss Slessor, the people here trust you more than anyone. Will you be our {vice-consul} and judge their cases?"},{"sc":"palaver","n":"So Mary became a {magistrate}, the first woman ever to be appointed as one anywhere in the British Empire."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"She held court under the trees or on her veranda. People came with quarrels about land, debts, marriages and stolen goats."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"oldmary","e":"stern","t":"You stole his yams? Then you will work on his farm until they are paid back. And you two will shake hands and stop this foolishness."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"She judged with common sense, fairness and humour. She protected women, children and slaves, who had often had no one to stand up for them."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"She wasn't interested in power or titles. She just wanted people to be treated fairly, as God sees them."}],"quote":{"text":"He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?","who":"Micah 6:8","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Justice with mercy","text":"As a judge, Mary was fair but kind, firm but full of common sense. She protected the weakest. Leaders who do justice and love mercy, and stay humble, make communities safe and strong."},"think":"If you were a judge, how would you balance being fair with being kind?","quiz":[{"q":"Who asked Mary to be vice-consul?","o":["Sir Claude MacDonald","Queen Victoria","Chief Edem","Her mother"],"why":"The British government's chief officer."},{"q":"What was historic about Mary becoming a magistrate?","o":["She was the first woman appointed one in the British Empire","She was the youngest","She was paid a lot","She was a queen"],"why":"In 1892."},{"q":"Where did she hold court?","o":["Under trees or on her veranda","In a palace","On a ship","In London"],"why":"Close to the people."},{"q":"Who did she especially protect?","o":["Women, children and slaves","Only chiefs","Only the rich","Nobody"],"why":"The weakest."},{"q":"What three things does Micah 6:8 say God requires?","o":["Do justly, love mercy, walk humbly","Be rich, be famous, be strong","Eat, sleep, play","Win, fight, rule"],"why":"Mary lived them out."}]},"ms4s2":{"story":[{"sc":"hut","n":"Mary never married. But she was never alone. Her house was always full of children."},{"sc":"hut","n":"There were rescued twins, orphans, and children nobody else wanted. Over the years, she adopted many of them as her own: Janie, Mary, Alice, Maggie, Dan and others."},{"sc":"hut","w":"msjanie","e":"big","t":"Ma! The baby's crying again, Dan has lost his shoe, and the goat has eaten the soap!"},{"sc":"hut","w":"oldmary","e":"big","t":"Ha! Never a dull moment in this house! Janie, bring the baby. Dan, look under the bed!"},{"sc":"hut","n":"It was noisy, busy and full of laughter. The older children helped care for the younger ones. Mary taught them to read, to work and to pray."},{"sc":"hut","w":"msdan","e":"smile","t":"Ma, sing to us before we sleep!"},{"sc":"hut","n":"To the people around her, Mary was 'Ma'. But to these children, she was truly Mother. God had given the mill girl from Dundee a very big family."}],"quote":{"text":"God setteth the solitary in families.","who":"Psalm 68:6","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Family is bigger","text":"Mary had no husband or children of her own birth, but God gave her a huge family of rescued children. Family isn't only about blood. It's about love, belonging and caring for those who need a home."},"think":"Who in your life is 'family' even though you're not related by blood?","quiz":[{"q":"Was Mary ever alone at home?","o":["Never: her house was full of children","Always","Only on Sundays","Only at night"],"why":"A very big family."},{"q":"Which of these was one of Mary's adopted children?","o":["Dan","Etim","Ursus","Ajayi"],"why":"Along with Janie, Mary, Alice and Maggie."},{"q":"What was life like in Mary's house?","o":["Noisy, busy and full of laughter","Silent and sad","Strict and cold","Empty"],"why":"Never a dull moment!"},{"q":"What did Mary teach her children?","o":["To read, to work and to pray","To fight","To be lazy","Nothing"],"why":"She raised them well."},{"q":"What does Psalm 68:6 say God does for the lonely?","o":["Sets them in families","Leaves them alone","Forgets them","Punishes them"],"why":"God setteth the solitary in families."}]},"ms4s3":{"story":[{"sc":"sickroom","n":"Life in the forest was hard on Mary's body. Again and again, she was struck down by {malaria} and other fevers."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"Several times she became so ill that she had to sail back to Scotland to recover. Each time, as soon as she was strong enough, she returned to Calabar."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"While she was in Africa, her mother and her sisters died in Scotland. Mary was heartbroken. Now there was almost no one waiting for her there."},{"sc":"sickroom","w":"oldmary","e":"worried","t":"My mother and sisters are gone. But heaven is nearer to me now than Scotland. Nobody will worry if I go further up-country."},{"sc":"sickroom","n":"Instead of making her give up, her grief made her braver. She leaned on God more than ever."},{"sc":"hut","w":"msjanie","e":"worried","t":"Ma, you're so thin. Please rest."},{"sc":"hut","w":"oldmary","e":"smile","t":"I'll rest when the work is done, my dear. The Lord gives me strength for each day."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Her body grew weaker over the years. But her spirit grew stronger. Day by day, God renewed her."}],"quote":{"text":"For which cause we faint not; but though our outward man perish, yet the inward man is renewed day by day.","who":"2 Corinthians 4:16","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Strength for each day","text":"Mary was often sick and grieving, but she didn't give up. God gave her strength one day at a time. When you're weak or sad, you don't need strength for your whole life at once, just for today."},"think":"Mary said heaven felt nearer than Scotland. What do you think she meant?","quiz":[{"q":"What illness often struck Mary?","o":["Malaria and other fevers","A broken leg","Toothache","Chickenpox"],"why":"Life in the forest was hard."},{"q":"What did Mary do when she recovered in Scotland?","o":["Returned to Calabar","Stayed in Scotland for ever","Went to India","Retired"],"why":"As soon as she was strong enough."},{"q":"Who died in Scotland while Mary was in Africa?","o":["Her mother and sisters","Queen Victoria","David Livingstone","Janie"],"why":"She was heartbroken."},{"q":"How did grief change Mary?","o":["It made her braver and lean on God more","She gave up","She became angry","She forgot God"],"why":"Heaven felt nearer."},{"q":"What does 2 Corinthians 4:16 say is renewed day by day?","o":["The inward man","Our money","Our clothes","Our houses"],"why":"God renews our spirit."}]},"ms4s4":{"story":[{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"By 1903, Okoyong had changed. There were churches, schools and peace. Other people could carry on the work there."},{"sc":"calabarriver","w":"oldmary","e":"big","t":"Okoyong doesn't need me now. There are people further up the river who have never heard of Jesus. I must go to them."},{"sc":"niger","n":"Although she was in her fifties and often unwell, Mary moved further inland, to {Itu} and the creeks and villages beyond, among the Ibibio people."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"She walked through forests, travelled by canoe, opened new schools and churches, and kept rescuing children."},{"sc":"hut","n":"She also helped start a place where women and girls, especially mothers of twins and girls with nowhere to go, could live safely, learn skills and earn their own living."},{"sc":"hut","w":"twinmother","e":"smile","t":"Ma, here I can work and feed my children. Nobody can chase me away."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"Mary could have retired. Instead, she kept pressing on. She always wanted to reach one more village."}],"quote":{"text":"I press toward the mark for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus.","who":"Philippians 3:14","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Keep pressing on","text":"When Okoyong was settled, Mary didn't relax. She went further, to people who still needed help. She also helped women stand on their own feet. Leaders keep their eyes on those still waiting."},"think":"Why do you think Mary helped women learn skills to earn their own living? How does that help a whole community?","quiz":[{"q":"What had Okoyong become by 1903?","o":["A place with churches, schools and peace","A ruined place","A big city","Empty"],"why":"Others could carry on."},{"q":"Where did Mary go next?","o":["Further inland to Itu and beyond","Back to Scotland","To Lagos","To London"],"why":"Among the Ibibio people."},{"q":"How old was Mary when she moved inland again?","o":["In her fifties","Twenty","Ninety","Twelve"],"why":"And often unwell."},{"q":"What did she help start for women and girls?","o":["A place to live safely, learn skills and earn a living","A prison","A market only","A palace"],"why":"Especially mothers of twins."},{"q":"What does Philippians 3:14 say Paul did?","o":["Pressed toward the mark","Gave up","Sat down","Went home"],"why":"Keep pressing on."}]}}},"ms5":{"challenge":[{"q":"How did Mary visit villages in her last years?","o":["In a wheeled chair pushed by her children","By helicopter","On a camel","She didn't"],"why":"Carried by love."},{"q":"In which village did Mary die?","o":["Use Ikot Oku","Ekenge","Osogun","Duke Town"],"why":"In today's Akwa Ibom State."},{"q":"For how long had Mary served in Nigeria?","o":["Nearly forty years","Two years","Seventy years","Ten years"],"why":"From 1876 to 1915."},{"q":"What did the government give her?","o":["A funeral with honours","A palace","A ship","Nothing"],"why":"An official funeral."},{"q":"Who lined the way at her funeral?","o":["Chiefs, market women, twin mothers and rescued children","Only soldiers","Only missionaries","Nobody"],"why":"Many wept."},{"q":"In which country is Mary a national hero?","o":["Scotland","France","Brazil","Japan"],"why":"And loved in Nigeria too."},{"q":"In which Nigerian state is Calabar?","o":["Cross River","Lagos","Kano","Oyo"],"why":"Where Mary is buried."},{"q":"What changed about how twins are seen?","o":["They became a blessing, not a curse","Nothing","They became kings","They were banned"],"why":"A big change that Mary helped begin."},{"q":"What does 'Ma' mean?","o":["Mother","Madam","Mighty","Mistress"],"why":"A mother to everyone."},{"q":"Complete the verse: \"I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the ___.\"","o":["faith","money","peace","secret"],"why":"2 Timothy 4:7."}],"bonus":{"q":"BONUS! Put Mary's life in order: which came FIRST?","o":["Working in the jute mill","Becoming a judge","Moving to Okoyong","Adopting Janie"],"why":"At eleven years old in Dundee."},"choices":[{"title":"Caring for the old","q":"Your grandmother is getting old and slow. She asks you to walk with her to the market, but your friends are going to play football.","o":[{"t":"Walk with Grandma, enjoy her stories, and join your friends later.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary's children carried her with love in her old age."},{"t":"Say you're too busy.","f":"One day you'll be glad you went."},{"t":"Go, but complain the whole way.","f":"Love isn't just doing, it's how you do it."}]},{"title":"Chasing fame","q":"Your friends are trying to go viral online by doing silly, risky stunts. They ask you to join.","o":[{"t":"Say no, and look for ways to make a real difference instead.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! Mary never chased fame, yet she's remembered for love."},{"t":"Join in. Fame is fame.","f":"Fame without goodness fades fast."},{"t":"Do an even riskier stunt.","f":"Your life is worth far more than views."}]},{"title":"Your Calabar","q":"You notice that a group of children in your area can't afford school books. You want to help, but you're 'just a kid'.","o":[{"t":"Talk to a parent or teacher, start a small book drive, and invite friends to help.","best":true,"f":"Best choice! A mill girl changed Calabar. You can start small."},{"t":"Do nothing. Someone else will.","f":"Maybe God put you there for a reason."},{"t":"Post about it but don't act.","f":"Mary acted, not just talked."}]}],"superpower":[{"sc":"rainforest","n":"SUPERPOWER! Here's how you'll use Mary Slessor's story for the rest of your life."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"🛞 Love given freely comes back. Care for those who once cared for you."},{"sc":"hut","n":"🌺 Finish well. Keep the faith to the end."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"💷 Don't chase fame. Love well, and leave a blessed memory."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"🌍 Ask God, 'Who is my Calabar?' Then start, even small."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"👏 Well done! You've finished the story of Ma Slessor."}],"stops":{"ms5s1":{"story":[{"sc":"rainforest","n":"In her last years, Mary was too weak to walk long distances. But she still wanted to visit the villages."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"So she travelled in a light wheeled chair, and her children and helpers pushed and pulled her along the forest paths."},{"sc":"rainforest","w":"msdan","e":"big","t":"Hold on, Ma! Here comes a bumpy bit!"},{"sc":"rainforest","w":"oldmary","e":"big","t":"Ha! Gently, Dan! Your old Ma isn't a sack of yams!"},{"sc":"palaver","n":"The children she had once rescued were now carrying her. The mother was being cared for by her children."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Even in her sixties, she taught, judged quarrels, cared for the sick and kept welcoming children who had nowhere to go."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"She had given her life to them. Now, in her weakness, they gave their love back to her."}],"quote":{"text":"And even to your old age I am he; and even to hoar hairs will I carry you.","who":"Isaiah 46:4","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Love comes back","text":"The children Mary once carried were now carrying her. Love given freely often comes back in surprising ways. And God promises to carry us all the way, even to old age."},"think":"How do you think Mary's children felt, being able to care for the woman who had saved them?","quiz":[{"q":"How did Mary travel in her last years?","o":["In a wheeled chair pushed by her children","By car","On horseback","She stayed home"],"why":"She still wanted to visit the villages."},{"q":"Who pushed and pulled her?","o":["Her children and helpers","Soldiers","Oxen","Nobody"],"why":"The rescued now carried their rescuer."},{"q":"What did Mary joke she wasn't?","o":["A sack of yams","A queen","A goat","A judge"],"why":"She kept her humour."},{"q":"What did she keep doing in her sixties?","o":["Teaching, judging and caring for children","Nothing","Travelling to Europe","Building ships"],"why":"She never stopped serving."},{"q":"What does Isaiah 46:4 promise?","o":["God will carry us even to old age","Old age is lonely","God leaves the old","Nothing"],"why":"Even to hoar hairs."}]},"ms5s2":{"story":[{"sc":"hut","n":"In January 1915, at the village of {Use Ikot Oku}, Mary fell ill with fever one last time."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Her adopted children gathered round her bed. They nursed her, prayed with her and sang to her."},{"sc":"hut","w":"msjanie","e":"worried","t":"Ma, please don't leave us."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Mary had spent her whole life telling them about Jesus. Now she was ready to go home to him."},{"sc":"night","n":"Early in the morning of 13 January 1915, Mary Slessor died, aged sixty-six. She had served in Nigeria for nearly forty years."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"Her body was carried down the river to Calabar. The government gave her an official funeral with honours."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"Crowds lined the way: chiefs, market women, twin mothers, freed slaves, children she had rescued. Many wept openly."},{"sc":"calabarriver","w":"twinmother","e":"worried","t":"Our Ma is gone. Who will ever love us like she did?"},{"sc":"church","n":"She was buried in Calabar, in the land she loved, among the people she called her own."}],"quote":{"text":"I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith.","who":"2 Timothy 4:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Finish well","text":"Mary served for nearly forty years and kept the faith to the end. Starting well matters, but finishing well matters even more. Every day of faithfulness adds up to a life that finishes well."},"think":"What do you think it means to 'finish well'? What would you want people to say about you at the end?","quiz":[{"q":"When did Mary Slessor die?","o":["13 January 1915","2 December 1848","September 1876","29 June 1864"],"why":"At Use Ikot Oku."},{"q":"How old was she?","o":["Sixty-six","Twenty-seven","Ninety","Forty"],"why":"After nearly forty years in Nigeria."},{"q":"Who was with her at the end?","o":["Her adopted children","Nobody","Queen Victoria","Her father"],"why":"They nursed and sang to her."},{"q":"Where was she buried?","o":["Calabar","Dundee","London","Edinburgh"],"why":"In the land she loved."},{"q":"What three things does Paul say in 2 Timothy 4:7?","o":["Fought a good fight, finished my course, kept the faith","Ate, slept, rested","Won, lost, quit","Ran, hid, escaped"],"why":"Finish well."}]},"ms5s3":{"story":[{"sc":"letterdesk","n":"The same year she died, a writer named W. P. Livingstone published a book about her life. It became famous around the world. This story is based on it."},{"sc":"calabarriver","n":"In Calabar today, people still speak of 'Ma Slessor'. Her name is on streets, schools and buildings. Her grave is visited and honoured."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"In Scotland, she is a national hero. Dundee, the city where she worked in the mill, remembers her proudly."},{"sc":"dundee","n":"Her picture was even printed on Scottish ten-pound banknotes. Imagine: the poor mill girl who once had hardly any money, on the money itself!"},{"sc":"palaver","n":"And in Nigeria today, twins are no longer seen as a curse but as a blessing. Many brave Nigerians worked for that change. Mary helped it begin."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"efikchild","e":"big","t":"My grandma told me that Ma Slessor helped save twins like my mum and her sister!"},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"Mary didn't seek fame. She would probably laugh at being on a banknote. She just wanted to love people the way Jesus loves them."}],"quote":{"text":"The memory of the just is blessed.","who":"Proverbs 10:7","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"A blessed memory","text":"Mary didn't try to be remembered, but she is: in Calabar and in Scotland. People remember those who loved them sacrificially. The best way to be remembered is not to chase fame, but to love well."},"think":"Why do you think people in two different countries still remember and honour Mary Slessor?","quiz":[{"q":"Who wrote a famous book about Mary's life?","o":["W. P. Livingstone","David Livingstone","John Bunyan","Mary herself"],"why":"Published in 1915."},{"q":"What do people in Calabar still call her?","o":["Ma Slessor","Queen Mary","The Red Lady","Teacher"],"why":"Her name is on streets and schools."},{"q":"Where was her picture printed in Scotland?","o":["On ten-pound banknotes","On stamps only","On football shirts","Nowhere"],"why":"The mill girl on the money!"},{"q":"How are twins seen in much of Nigeria today?","o":["As a blessing","As a curse","As a problem","As strangers"],"why":"Mary helped that change begin."},{"q":"What does Proverbs 10:7 say?","o":["The memory of the just is blessed","Nobody remembers anyone","Only kings are remembered","Forget the past"],"why":"Love well."}]},"ms5s4":{"story":[{"sc":"jutemill","n":"Let's go back to the beginning. A poor girl in a noisy mill, with a book propped on her loom and a dream in her heart."},{"sc":"jutemill","n":"She wasn't rich. She wasn't highly educated. She had a hard home. She had red hair that made people stare. She was just Mary."},{"sc":"rainforest","n":"But she said 'yes' to God. And God used that 'yes' to rescue hundreds of children, stop wars, end cruel customs and change a whole region."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"You might not sail to a faraway place. But God has a 'Calabar' for you too: a place, a group of people, a need that he wants you to care about."},{"sc":"palaver","n":"Maybe it's lonely children in your school. Maybe it's girls who can't go to school. Maybe it's the sick, the poor or people who have never heard about Jesus."},{"sc":"palaver","w":"oldmary","e":"smile","t":"If God can use a mill girl from Dundee, he can use you. Say yes to him, and see what he does."},{"sc":"throneglory","n":"Mary Slessor: mill girl, missionary, twin rescuer, peacemaker, judge, mother of many. Ma."}],"quote":{"text":"Learn to do well; seek judgment, relieve the oppressed, judge the fatherless, plead for the widow.","who":"Isaiah 1:17","tag":"From the Bible"},"lesson":{"title":"Find your Calabar","text":"God used an ordinary girl who said yes. He has a place and people for you to love too. Ask him: 'Who is my Calabar?' Then start, even small, today."},"think":"Who might God be calling you to care about? What is one small step you could take this week?","quiz":[{"q":"What was Mary like at the beginning of her story?","o":["A poor mill girl with a hard home","A rich princess","A famous scholar","A soldier"],"why":"Just Mary."},{"q":"What did God do with Mary's 'yes'?","o":["Rescued children, stopped wars and changed a region","Nothing much","Made her rich","Made her a queen"],"why":"An ordinary girl, an extraordinary God."},{"q":"What does 'your Calabar' mean in this story?","o":["The place or people God wants you to care about","A holiday","A city you must move to","A kind of food"],"why":"God has one for you."},{"q":"What did Mary say God can do?","o":["Use you, if you say yes","Nothing","Only use missionaries","Only use adults"],"why":"Say yes and see."},{"q":"Which of these does Isaiah 1:17 tell us to do?","o":["Relieve the oppressed","Ignore the poor","Get rich","Stay comfortable"],"why":"Learn to do well."}]}}}});
